# ==============================================================================
# Data - regenerable, and far too large for git
# ==============================================================================
# The full corpus is ~1.2 GB of raw archives plus ~120 MB of canonical parquet.
# All of it is reproducible from public sources with two commands, so the pipeline
# is version-controlled and the bytes are not:
#
#   python -m reip.ingest.aemo                    # ~1 GB from nemweb.com.au
#   python -m reip.ingest.build_corpus ...        # weather from open-meteo.com
#
data/*
!data/canonical/
data/canonical/*
!data/canonical/balance_*.json
!data/canonical/seasonal_*.json
!data/canonical/ensemble_*.npz
!data/.gitkeep

# Third-party datasets dropped in by hand. Licensing varies by source and none of
# it is ours to redistribute.
*.zip
*.xlsx
*.nc
*.grib
*.grib2
*.om

# HTTP response caches from the ingest modules
.cache/

# ==============================================================================
# Model artifacts
# ==============================================================================
# The six boosted-tree models are ~160 MB and change on every retrain, so committing
# them would add 160 MB to history each time and quickly dwarf the source. They are
# deterministic given the corpus and `random_seed`, and rebuilt with:
#
#   python -m reip.models.train
#
# The metadata sidecars are kept: a few kilobytes each, and they are the record of
# what was trained - feature order, split composition, calibration constant, and the
# unseen-site scores. Reviewing a model change means reading these, not the trees.
artifacts/*
!artifacts/.gitkeep
!artifacts/*_metadata.json
# Calibration constants, not trained weights: small, text, and required to reproduce a
# served forecast. Losing them would silently change what the platform outputs.
!artifacts/scenario_dispersion.json
!artifacts/balance_calibration.json

# ==============================================================================
# Reports
# ==============================================================================
# Benchmarks and figures ARE committed: they are the evidence behind the accuracy
# claims in the README, they are small, and a reviewer should be able to see them
# in a diff when a model changes. Only scratch comparisons are excluded.
reports/*_scratch*
reports/tmp/

# ==============================================================================
# Scratch output from development
# ==============================================================================
*.log
probe_*.json
*_probe.json
scratch/
notebooks/scratch/

